                <pb facs="0561" n="467" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg467"/>

                <head>No. 313.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. White</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-03-18"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 18,
                        1887</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 570.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your dispatch No. 459, of the 5th instant,
                    communicating to the Department the intelligence that one George F. Anderson,
                    lately engaged in the fictitious estates business, was found guilty on trial of
                    obtaining money under false pretenses and sentenced to five years’ penal
                    servitude, has been received and read with satisfaction. The contents of your
                    dispatch will be given to the press for the information of the public.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
